data.borrow-locals-on-the-host#Borrow locals:
Both independent programs on this page use these particle types.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 The names match the core simulation. The explicit-capture variant uses the same Float32 representation for device-compatible arithmetic; see device eligibility.
The lexical stage defines move inside Move.update and passes it as a value.
@fieldwise_init struct Move(System): """Counts particles through a sequential host closure.""" def update(mut self, mut context: SystemContext[...]) raises: """Moves the particles and observes a synchronous captured result. Args: context: Access to the particle world. Raises: Error: If resources, execution, or the visit check fail. """ var scale: Float32 = 2 var visits = 0 def move( rows: KernelContext[ Filter().include[Position]().read[Velocity](), Resources().read[Time](), ] ) {imm scale, mut visits}: """Moves each row and counts it through a host borrow. Args: rows: One matching physical store's rows and time. """ ref time = rows.resources.get[Time]() for particle in rows: ref position = particle.get[Position]() ref velocity = particle.get[Velocity]() position.x += velocity.dx * time.delta * scale position.y += velocity.dy * time.delta * scale visits += 1 context.run(move) assert_equal(visits, 2) imm scale borrows the local input and mut visits borrows the counter. context.run(move) selects the value-taking overload, which supports only sequential HOST. Every matching physical store receives a call, so visits increments inside the row loop. Completion is synchronous; its final value is visible at the assertion. Do not capture an alias that structurally changes the world while rows are borrowed.
The driver checks two matching physical stores with different compositions.
def main() raises: """Checks the closure across two matching physical stores. Raises: Error: If setup, execution, or a position check fails. """ var world = World[Position, Velocity, Int32]() world.resources.add(Time(0.25)) var walker = world.storage.add_entity(Position(0, 0), Velocity(1, 0)) var climber = world.storage.add_entity( Position(5, 0), Velocity(0, 2), Int32(1) ) var scheduler = Scheduler[Position, Velocity, Int32](world^) scheduler.add_system(Move()) scheduler.run(steps=1) assert_almost_equal(scheduler.world.storage.read[Position](walker).x, 0.5) assert_almost_equal(scheduler.world.storage.read[Position](climber).y, 1.0) print("PASS: lexical host captures across two stores") walker and climber both move once, and the counter counts rows across both calls. The extra Int32 component puts climber in another logical archetype; it does not change the movement filter. Run pixi run mojo run -I src examples/particles/lexical_closure.mojo from the repository root to check the stage.

data.keep-the-kernel-independent-of-transfer-:
The stage uses plain particle types and the same read-only time resource.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 Time is one resource value; Position and Velocity have separate columns in each physical store. Reuse tracks those units, not individual scalar fields.
The kernel remains unchanged when you enable reuse.
comptime move_filter = Filter().include[Position]().read[Velocity]() comptime move_resources = Resources().read[Time]() def move(context: KernelContext[move_filter, move_resources]): """Moves each row using its velocity and the shared step length. Args: context: Matching rows and read-only time. """ ref time = context.resources.get[Time]() for particle in context: ref position = particle.get[Position]() ref velocity = particle.get[Velocity]() position.x += velocity.dx * time.delta position.y += velocity.dy * time.delta Position is readable and writable, Velocity and Time are read-only. A successful device call makes host and device copies current even for the written Position, so the next identical call may skip its upload too. Written output still downloads after every run; this is not persistent unsynchronized device state.
The system chooses the target independently of the world’s transfer policy.
@fieldwise_init struct Move[backend: ExecutionBackend](System): """Keeps the movement kernel and target independent of copy policy.""" def update(mut self, mut context: SystemContext[...]) raises: """Completes movement and eager copy-back. Args: context: Access to the simulation world. Raises: Error: If preparation or execution fails. """ context.run[move, backend=Self.backend]() Both default-transfer and reuse runs execute the same Move specialization. This keeps the comparison’s semantic work equal.

data.write-a-separate-output-for-each-particl:
The stage uses the same particle types and shared time.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 A resource or mutable capture would be one shared value for every worker. The additional Displacement below instead belongs to each entity.
Write all output fields before a later step reads them.
@fieldwise_init struct Displacement(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """A private output for one particle, avoiding a shared kernel counter.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 comptime move_filter = Filter().include[Position]().read[Velocity]().write[ Displacement ]() comptime move_resources = Resources().read[Time]() def move(context: KernelContext[move_filter, move_resources]): """Writes every per-row output before a later single-caller reduction. Args: context: Particle inputs and set-only displacement output. """ ref time = context.resources.get[Time]() for particle in context: ref position = particle.get[Position]() ref velocity = particle.get[Velocity]() var dx = velocity.dx * time.delta var dy = velocity.dy * time.delta position.x += dx position.y += dy particle.set(Displacement(dx, dy)) The filter grants read-write Position, read-only Velocity, and set-only Displacement. Every visited row initializes its output. The same row-local work can satisfy device/threaded restrictions without a shared increment.
Reduce the completed output on the coordinating host caller.
@fieldwise_init struct Move(System): """Runs row work before a single-caller reduction.""" def update(mut self, mut context: SystemContext[...]) raises: """Reduces completed per-row outputs without a shared kernel write. Args: context: Access to the world after synchronous kernel completion. Raises: Error: If execution or the reduction check fails. """ context.run[move]() var total = Displacement(0, 0) for particle in context.world[].storage.query[ Filter().read[Displacement]() ](): ref value = particle.get[Displacement]() total.x += value.x total.y += value.y assert_almost_equal(total.x, 9.25) assert_almost_equal(total.y, 18.5) The query starts after synchronous kernel completion. One caller sums all 37 outputs, obtaining (9.25, 18.5); there is no concurrent write to total. For a fully parallel reduction, use disjoint partial-output components and later passes with explicit dependencies. A single designated writer is also valid when it need not read unfinished work from other threads, as in the capture count example. A thread-zero branch alone is not a global barrier.

data.keep-inputs-portable-and-work-independen:
The stage uses plain components and explicitly read-only time.
@fieldwise_init struct Position(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle coordinates, using device-compatible scalar arithmetic.""" var x: Float32 var y: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Velocity(Copyable, Movable, TrivialRegisterPassable): """Particle displacement per unit of simulated time.""" var dx: Float32 var dy: Float32 @fieldwise_init struct Time(Copyable, Movable): """Shared simulated step length.""" var delta: Float32 The component fields contain only scalar values. Threaded eligibility checks pointer-free trivial representation for accessed components; a device component trait alone does not establish this. Unaccessed owning components can coexist in the world.
The movement loop changes only its own row.
comptime move_filter = Filter().include[Position]().read[Velocity]() comptime move_resources = Resources().read[Time]() def move(context: KernelContext[move_filter, move_resources]): """Moves each row using its velocity and the shared step length. Args: context: Matching rows and read-only time. """ ref time = context.resources.get[Time]() for particle in context: ref position = particle.get[Position]() ref velocity = particle.get[Velocity]() position.x += velocity.dx * time.delta position.y += velocity.dy * time.delta Resources().read[Time]() shares one immutable time value with every chunk. Ordinary mutable resources, including the Resources[Time]() read-write default, are rejected even when a configuration uses sequential fallback. Adapter views can expose owning read inputs.
Opt in per invocation; configuring the world alone does not enable threading.
@fieldwise_init struct Move[threaded: Bool](System): """Opts into host chunks for this movement kernel.""" def update(mut self, mut context: SystemContext[...]) raises: """Moves disjoint rows and joins before returning. Args: context: Access to the simulation world. Raises: Error: If threaded eligibility or preparation fails. """ context.run[move, threaded=Self.threaded]() context.run[move, threaded=True]() requires a nonraising thin kernel and HOST. Lexical closures, explicit captures, and threaded=True with DEVICE fail at compile time. EntitySelection.run supports the same threaded engine on exact selected ranges.

